faq:
  - q: "What is SpaceX's hiring process in 2026?"
    a: "SpaceX's hiring process is fast and technically intensive. General flow: (1) Application screen — SpaceX receives enormous volume; resume clarity and technical depth matter significantly at the first screen. (2) Recruiter screen — 20–30 minutes on background, role fit, and motivation. (3) Technical phone screen — a 60-minute technical interview covering domain-specific questions (aerospace, electrical, software, or manufacturing engineering). (4) Onsite interview — 4–6 rounds in one day covering technical depth, practical problem solving, and culture fit. SpaceX does not use many standard interview frameworks — expect hands-on technical questions tied directly to your domain, not abstract case studies. Decisions are typically made within 1–2 weeks of the onsite."
  - q: "What does SpaceX look for in candidates?"
    a: "SpaceX's culture filters for a specific profile: (1) Mission obsession — SpaceX's mission (making humanity multiplanetary) is not marketing; candidates who are not genuinely excited by the mission struggle in interviews. (2) Extreme ownership — SpaceX's teams are small relative to the scope of what they build. Engineers own systems from design to launch. The ability to hold responsibility under uncertainty and high stakes is a core hiring signal. (3) Speed of execution — like Tesla, SpaceX moves faster than traditional aerospace. Candidates with traditional government contractor backgrounds who are accustomed to slow decision cycles often struggle with SpaceX's pace. (4) Deep technical first principles — SpaceX is famous for rethinking aerospace from scratch. Candidates who demonstrate first-principles reasoning over learned-by-rote engineering patterns consistently outperform."
  - q: "What engineering roles exist at SpaceX and what do they require?"
    a: "SpaceX has several major engineering tracks: (1) Aerospace / Propulsion / Structures — core rocket engineering. Requires deep domain expertise in propulsion, aerodynamics, structural analysis, or guidance, navigation, and control (GNC). Top aerospace graduate programs (Caltech, MIT, Stanford, University of Michigan, Georgia Tech) feed this track. (2) Avionics / Electrical — flight computer systems, power electronics, RF systems. Embedded systems expertise and hardware-software integration experience required. (3) Software Engineering — flight software, ground systems, simulation, and internal tooling. C++ and Python are dominant. The bar is high but different from FAANG — practical, embedded, and reliability-critical experience matters more than algorithm puzzles. (4) Manufacturing / Production — Starship and Falcon 9 manufacturing at scale. Process engineering, welding engineering, and composites manufacturing experience valued. (5) Starlink — satellite engineering, RF/antenna systems, network engineering, and embedded software for the world's largest satellite constellation."
  - q: "How does SpaceX evaluate technical candidates in interviews?"
    a: "SpaceX technical interviews are domain-specific and practical. For aerospace engineers: expect to derive equations, defend design choices, and walk through specific failure scenarios in your domain (propulsion, structures, GNC). SpaceX interviewers will push until you reach the edge of your knowledge — knowing where that edge is and being honest about it is a strength. For software engineers: expect systems design and C++ or Python implementation questions, with an emphasis on reliability, safety-critical software patterns, and embedded systems awareness. For manufacturing: expect process improvement case studies, tolerance analysis, and real manufacturing trade-off discussions. SpaceX does not use LeetCode-heavy screens — practical knowledge of your domain is evaluated."
  - q: "What does SpaceX pay in 2026?"
    a: "SpaceX compensation is below FAANG and below its direct tech competitors. The company trades on mission and impact, and candidates accept below-market pay for the opportunity. Aerospace Engineer (entry): $85–110K. Senior Aerospace Engineer: $120–160K. Software Engineer: $120–180K TC. Senior Software Engineer: $180–240K TC. Lead/Principal Engineer: $220–320K TC. Manufacturing/Production: $70–120K. SpaceX offers stock options (not RSUs) — value is tied to SpaceX's private valuation, which has grown dramatically with each funding round, but liquidity is limited and uncertain. For candidates who join early and stay long, equity has been meaningful. SpaceX's culture makes clear that mission, not compensation, is the primary value proposition."
  - q: "Do I need a security clearance to work at SpaceX?"
    a: "Not for most roles, but it helps for some. SpaceX holds US government contracts (NASA, DoD, Space Force) that require cleared personnel for specific programs. ITAR compliance means some roles are restricted to US persons (citizens and permanent residents). A small subset of roles — particularly those supporting classified national security missions — require active SECRET or TS clearances. However, the majority of SpaceX engineering roles on Falcon 9, Dragon, and Starship commercial programs do not require a clearance. SpaceX will sponsor candidates for clearances when required for specific roles. Non-US citizens can work at SpaceX in non-ITAR roles, but the most technically interesting programs are ITAR-restricted."
