      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">SpaceX's culture and the hiring filter</h2>
      <p>SpaceX is not a conventional aerospace company or a tech company. It is a vertically integrated launch and spacecraft manufacturer operating at a pace and ambition level that has no direct peer. Understanding the culture is prerequisite to preparing effectively.</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Small teams, massive scope.</strong> SpaceX builds rockets with engineering teams that are a fraction of the size of traditional aerospace. Each engineer owns significantly more scope than at Boeing, Lockheed, or even NASA. In interviews, demonstrate that you can own systems, not just contribute to them.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure is acceptable; slow is not.</strong> SpaceX's test-to-failure philosophy means rapid iteration is valued over guaranteed success on the first attempt. Candidates from government aerospace who describe cautious, committee-driven processes struggle — candidates who describe fast iteration, learned failure, and rapid response do not.</li>
        <li><strong>The mission is evaluated directly.</strong> SpaceX interviewers will ask why you want to work there. Generic answers ("cool rockets," "Elon," "tech challenge") fail. Specific, substantive answers — what aspect of making humanity multiplanetary motivates you, what you know about the Starship development program, why reusability matters — succeed.</li>
        <li><strong>Be honest about what you don't know.</strong> SpaceX interviewers push deep. When you hit the edge of your knowledge, say so clearly and demonstrate how you would figure it out. Candidates who bluff are spotted and filtered.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Preparing for SpaceX by engineering track</h2>
      <h3>Aerospace / Propulsion / GNC</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Review your graduate coursework in propulsion (Isp, thrust, nozzle design), structures (FEA, fatigue, fracture), or GNC (control systems, trajectory optimization) — be ready to derive and defend</li>
        <li>Know Raptor engine basics (full-flow staged combustion, methane fuel choice) and Starship design rationale — understanding SpaceX's specific engineering choices signals genuine engagement</li>
        <li>Prepare a technical deep-dive on a project you owned: what the requirements were, what tradeoffs you made, what failed, and what you changed</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Software Engineering</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Flight software, real-time systems, and safety-critical software patterns matter more than LeetCode grinding at SpaceX</li>
        <li>C++ proficiency (multithreading, memory management, real-time constraints) is expected for flight software roles; Python for simulation and tooling</li>
        <li>System design: fault-tolerant distributed systems, redundancy and failover strategies relevant to flight computer architectures</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Starlink (Network / RF / Embedded)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>RF systems, phased array antenna design, and satellite link budget analysis are core to Starlink hardware roles</li>
